>
> Here I implemented postpositions to the experimental grammar.
>
> {do ca fa prami mi co fe} = {fa do prami fe mi} = {do prami mi}
> {i lo vanci co ca klama fa mi} = {i ca lo vanci cu klama fa mi}
>
> Justification:
>
> 1. It's afterthought. The same reason people prefer GIhEKs to GEKs. One 
> may first say {lo vanci} and only then think what sumtcita matches their 
> thought.
> 2. Some languages like Japanese use postpositions. Some languages like 
> most SAE languages use case suffixes, not prefixes. This is what {ko'a co 
> FA} matches more closely.
>

Besides, "... ago" would be {copuza} since it's also a postposition.



> I think, this addition doesn't add any new scoping opportunities to the 
> language ( I mean scoping of {da}, tags, some UI).
>
> Objections to the github changelog? 
>

